WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:03.133
-I haven't been able to
congratulate you

00:00:03.133 --> 00:00:05.667
on winning your Academy Award
for "Judy," by the way.

00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:08.100
[ Cheers and applause ]
You were unbelievable

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.033
playing Judy Garland.

00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:14.467
You were phenomenal
in that as well.

00:00:14.467 --> 00:00:16.934
What do you remember about
winning the Oscar?

00:00:16.934 --> 00:00:19.367
Do you get excited for
these awards?

00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:21.267
-You know -- Well, yeah.

00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:22.934
I mean, of course, it's kind of
like you think

00:00:22.934 --> 00:00:24.534
it's a once-in-a-lifetime
experience.

00:00:24.534 --> 00:00:27.033
You want to pay attention and I
always try to, you know --

00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:28.434
I want to be grounded
when I go in.

00:00:28.434 --> 00:00:30.601
I just want to get in touch with
my family,

00:00:30.601 --> 00:00:33.801
talk to my mom, dad, my brother.
-That's good.

00:00:33.801 --> 00:00:36.334
-Do things that keep you
grounded before you go into it.

00:00:36.334 --> 00:00:38.734
-Don't get too big-headed
going in.

00:00:38.734 --> 00:00:40.200
-You know, keep your feet
on the ground

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.801
and just kind of enjoy
the moment.

00:00:41.801 --> 00:00:45.767
So I have a couple rituals
and things that I like to do

00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:48.567
before I go -- I like to walk.

00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:50.367
I don't like to get in the car,
you know, the long limo ride.

00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:51.734
-So you don't take
a limousine there?

00:00:51.734 --> 00:00:53.734
-Unh-unh, no, no, no,
'cause I don't like getting out

00:00:53.734 --> 00:00:55.467
and it's all crazy
and people screaming and stuff.

00:00:55.467 --> 00:00:57.200
So I sneak in.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.400
-You walk to the Oscars?
-I walk to the Oscars.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:01.667
-That's the greatest thing
I ever heard.

00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:03.167
-I sneak in, 'cause you know
all the people come out,

00:01:03.167 --> 00:01:05.300
and they stand and they're
looking at the red carpet,

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.000
so I come in
behind all the people.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.601
[ Laughter ]
-Wow!

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:11.334
-Yeah, I sneak in behind
all those -- So, this time,

00:01:11.334 --> 00:01:13.067
the only hotel room
that was available

00:01:13.067 --> 00:01:15.567
was a half a mile away.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:17.467
-You walked a half a mile
to the Oscars?

00:01:17.467 --> 00:01:20.033
-I did.
In shoes taller than this.

00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:21.801
And I don't recommend it.

00:01:21.801 --> 00:01:24.167
But it is a good exercise
for keeping you grounded.

00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:25.634
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, my God.

00:01:25.634 --> 00:01:28.434
You're like, "Why did I start
this tradition?"

00:01:28.434 --> 00:01:30.033
-Yeah, and it was raining
that night,

00:01:30.033 --> 00:01:31.901
so I was extra grounded
when I got there.

00:01:31.901 --> 00:01:33.634
-Oh, my gosh.
You were really grounded.

00:01:33.634 --> 00:01:35.167
-The girls know what
I'm talking about.

00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:37.300
You know, you do your hair
and then you're like, "Ohh."

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:38.968
[ Laughter ]

00:01:38.968 --> 00:01:40.567
-Did you get to party
afterwards?

00:01:40.567 --> 00:01:42.000
-Do you know what? Yeah, I did.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.601
I went back to the hotel and had
a little private dance party

00:01:45.601 --> 00:01:48.601
with my really good friends who
filled the room with balloons,

00:01:48.601 --> 00:01:51.367
and we ordered cheeseburgers,
as you do.

00:01:51.367 --> 00:01:54.167
And then we went to my pal
Guy Oseary's house.

00:01:54.167 --> 00:01:55.534
I've known him for --
You know Guy, right?

00:01:55.534 --> 00:01:56.968
-I love Guy Oseary.
-Guy Oseary, yeah.

00:01:56.968 --> 00:01:58.801
He's a music manager,
but when I met him,

00:01:58.801 --> 00:02:02.234
he was 17 years old and he was
interning at Maverick.

00:02:02.234 --> 00:02:05.100
-Yes.
-And every year now he hosts

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.267
this after party that,
if you can believe it,

00:02:07.267 --> 00:02:09.167
begins at 1:00 in the morning.

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:11.868
And you take your shoes off,
and nobody takes pictures.

00:02:11.868 --> 00:02:13.801
And he just turns the music up
the really loudly,

00:02:13.801 --> 00:02:15.200
and you just dance
in the living room.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.601
-That's exactly how you do it.
-Yeah, right?

00:02:16.601 --> 00:02:18.601
-That's what you're supposed
to do and have fun.

00:02:18.601 --> 00:02:20.901
-In that dress. Yeah, no shoes.

00:02:20.901 --> 00:02:22.400
Every year he does
something fun.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.467
One year, he had this photo
booth that you go into,

00:02:24.467 --> 00:02:26.601
and it takes pictures,
and then you get this book.

00:02:26.601 --> 00:02:28.567
It's kind of like an animation
where you flip it --

00:02:28.567 --> 00:02:29.934
the flip book, you know?
-Yeah.

00:02:29.934 --> 00:02:31.968
-Yeah, so you make this flip
book from the photographs.

00:02:31.968 --> 00:02:33.634
And last year he had
this photographer --

00:02:33.634 --> 00:02:35.100
or two years ago now,
my goodness --

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.667
he had a photographer named J.R.

00:02:36.667 --> 00:02:38.634
He takes a picture of you,
then he takes a picture of you

00:02:38.634 --> 00:02:40.367
with the picture --
life-size picture.

00:02:40.367 --> 00:02:42.634
Really cool. The cooler part
was, though,

00:02:42.634 --> 00:02:45.000
that Snoop Dogg was in the back.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:46.667
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:49.067
-It's always a fun party
when Snoop Dogg's there, yeah.

00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:50.734
-It's fun when Snoop Dogg's
anywhere.

00:02:50.734 --> 00:02:52.067
-Yeah, exactly. I agree.

00:02:52.067 --> 00:02:55.734
-Yeah, so, he asked if we could
take a picture together.

00:02:55.734 --> 00:02:58.601
Come on!
-You brought it to show tonight.

00:02:58.601 --> 00:02:59.934
Here's you and Snoop Dogg.

00:02:59.934 --> 00:03:01.634
Look at -- Not too shabby.

00:03:01.634 --> 00:03:02.968
I like that.
-Come on!

00:03:02.968 --> 00:03:05.100
[ Cheers and applause ]
-I would go see that movie.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.601
-Come on!
-I would watch this movie.

00:03:07.601 --> 00:03:08.667
-Right?
-Yeah.

00:03:08.667 --> 00:03:10.200
-I mean, right?
So, you go, you're like,

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.501
"It's Oscar night,"
but then it's, like, Snoop Dogg.

00:03:12.501 --> 00:03:15.033
-Yeah, that was a Snoop Dogg
night.

00:03:15.033 --> 00:03:16.601
-A great night.
It was a great night.

00:03:16.601 --> 00:03:18.934
-Did you stay grounded
when you were also nominated

00:03:18.934 --> 00:03:20.567
for a Grammy Award, by the way,
pal?

00:03:20.567 --> 00:03:22.100
Congratulations!
-Oh, that was crazy.

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:24.734
-I mean, come on.
-Thanks. Thank you.

00:03:24.734 --> 00:03:27.300
-Did you ever imagine in your
life, you go, "Oh, my gosh."

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.767
-No, not a chance.
You know, not a chance.

00:03:30.767 --> 00:03:32.434
Yeah, no, no, no.

00:03:32.434 --> 00:03:34.334
And with Harry Connick,
my buddy.

00:03:34.334 --> 00:03:36.601
And Rufus Wainwright,
who we actually did a duet.

00:03:36.601 --> 00:03:38.367
We did "Have Yourself
a Merry Little Christmas"

00:03:38.367 --> 00:03:41.067
on the "Judy" record.

00:03:41.067 --> 00:03:43.701
-You must have seen when Rufus
did "Rufus! Rufus! Rufus!"

00:03:43.701 --> 00:03:44.767
That concert?
-Oh, come on, I mean.

00:03:44.767 --> 00:03:46.234
-No, exactly, of course,
I mean, yeah.

00:03:46.234 --> 00:03:47.400
-He's extraordinary.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:48.901
-It was his tribute
to "Judy, Judy, Judy,"

00:03:48.901 --> 00:03:51.601
and who you just knocked it
out of the park with.

00:03:51.601 --> 00:03:53.400
-Thanks, Jimmy.
-But do you get intimidated when

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.267
you get in there with
Rufus Wainwright to sing?

00:03:55.267 --> 00:03:57.501
-Oh, gosh, yeah, you just got to
pretend really hard

00:03:57.501 --> 00:03:59.400
that you're doing okay,
you know?

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:02.100
-Yeah, I guess that's what
acting is. Yeah, I guess.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.834
-Yeah, some of the best
performances in life

00:04:04.834 --> 00:04:06.834
happen behind the scenes.
-Yeah.

00:04:06.834 --> 00:04:09.801
-No, that was really emotional
for me 'cause I have loved him.

00:04:09.801 --> 00:04:12.234
I mean, I've been singing duets
with him for 20 years

00:04:12.234 --> 00:04:15.801
in Laurel Canyon, in my car.
-Yes, on the speakers, yeah.

00:04:15.801 --> 00:04:17.367
In real life, you're like,
"Oh, my gosh."

00:04:17.367 --> 00:04:18.634
-Yeah, it was a big deal.
Big deal for me.

00:04:18.634 --> 00:04:22.868
-We're all excited here,
not just because it's NBC,

00:04:22.868 --> 00:04:26.767
but it is NBC tomorrow at 10:00
p.m., "The Thing About Pam."

00:04:26.767 --> 00:04:30.901
It is getting crazy-good
reviews, crazy-good buzz.

00:04:30.901 --> 00:04:33.334
One reporter was like, "Just
give her the Emmy right now.

00:04:33.334 --> 00:04:37.834
If this world has any sense to
it, please just give it to her.

00:04:37.834 --> 00:04:39.567
Renée Zellweger knocks it
out of the park."

00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:41.801
"The Thing About Pam" --
I love true crime.

00:04:41.801 --> 00:04:43.667
My wife loves true crime.

00:04:43.667 --> 00:04:45.167
We watch "Dateline."

00:04:45.167 --> 00:04:47.534
I try to make sure I don't watch
it right before I go to bed.

00:04:47.534 --> 00:04:48.467
-Right, right, right.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:48.467 --> 00:04:50.634
But inevitably, you do.
-You do.

00:04:50.634 --> 00:04:53.934
You do, and I have
pretty awesome nightmares.

00:04:53.934 --> 00:04:55.033
[ Laughter ]

00:04:55.033 --> 00:04:57.033
But I didn't know about
this podcast.

00:04:57.033 --> 00:04:59.033
-Mm-hmm.
-When you first heard --

00:04:59.033 --> 00:05:00.801
You got attached
when it was just a podcast?

00:05:00.801 --> 00:05:02.167
-Yeah, yeah, it was one of

00:05:02.167 --> 00:05:04.501
the most popular "Dateline"
episodes ever.

00:05:04.501 --> 00:05:05.667
I missed that one.

00:05:05.667 --> 00:05:08.067
But then, my partner,
Carmella Casinelli,

00:05:08.067 --> 00:05:10.000
at the Big Picture Co.,
sent me the podcast.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.167
And I listened to it
on the 5 freeway when I was

00:05:12.167 --> 00:05:13.934
taking Chester,
the rescue shepherd,

00:05:13.934 --> 00:05:16.200
up to get his hip replaced.
-Aww.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:19.634
-Yeah, and that was between the
Golden Globes and the Oscars.

00:05:19.634 --> 00:05:22.067
-Really keeping you grounded.
-It's the best.

00:05:22.067 --> 00:05:24.033
-How's Chester? Is he okay?
-He's rocking.

00:05:24.033 --> 00:05:26.267
Yeah, he's got his bionic hip,
and he's so cool.

00:05:26.267 --> 00:05:27.968
-That's awesome.
-He's so cool.

00:05:27.968 --> 00:05:29.901
But, yeah, so, I listened to it.
I binged it.

00:05:29.901 --> 00:05:31.734
And I thought,
"Man, that's so crazy."

00:05:31.734 --> 00:05:34.300
You just got started asking
yourself, "How, how, how?"

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:36.634
There's so much evidence
pointing away from

00:05:36.634 --> 00:05:41.133
this woman's husband, who was
actually convicted of murder.

00:05:41.133 --> 00:05:44.067
It's based on -- Well,
the podcast revolves around

00:05:44.067 --> 00:05:45.334
the story of Betsy Faria,

00:05:45.334 --> 00:05:48.400
the case of Betsy Faria's murder
in 2011.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:51.400
And her husband was convicted
and later exonerated.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:52.801
-And you play Pam?

00:05:52.801 --> 00:05:55.334
-I play Betsy's friend, Pam,
Pam Hupp, mm-hmm.

00:05:55.334 --> 00:05:57.200
-Yeah, and you go, "Yeah."

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:59.133
And then you go,
"It's the husband."

00:05:59.133 --> 00:06:01.234
It's like all these great true
crimes where you're like,

00:06:01.234 --> 00:06:03.234
"Oh, the husband did it."
-Yeah, "he did it." Yeah.

00:06:03.234 --> 00:06:05.234
-It's insurance,
that's what it is.

00:06:05.234 --> 00:06:06.767
Though what's even amazing?

00:06:06.767 --> 00:06:10.100
You got Keith Morrison from
"Dateline" to narrate the show.

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:11.367
Double whammy.

00:06:11.367 --> 00:06:12.634
I'm like, "What?!"

00:06:12.634 --> 00:06:14.434
Any super nerd is going to
freak out,

00:06:14.434 --> 00:06:16.667
'cause I love Keith Morrison.
-Love Keith Morrison.

00:06:16.667 --> 00:06:18.567
-Once I hear his voice I go,
"Oh, my God. No, no, no."

00:06:18.567 --> 00:06:20.834
-See, that's the fun thing.
You start sitting there,

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:23.200
you're like, "Hmm, I'm going to
call Rufus Wainwright."

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:26.934
-No, yeah, you get
Keith Morrison, why not?

00:06:26.934 --> 00:06:30.200
But you are unrecognizable
in this.

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:33.334
You have an accent --
a Missouri accent?

00:06:33.334 --> 00:06:34.701
-Yeah, a little bit, yeah.

00:06:34.701 --> 00:06:37.567
It's sort of ephemeral because
she moved around a lot.

00:06:37.567 --> 00:06:39.400
It was very specific to her,
so, you know --

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.067
-And you had crazy prosthetics,
amazing job.

00:06:42.067 --> 00:06:44.834
Who did the prosthetics?
-His name is Arian Titan, yeah.

00:06:44.834 --> 00:06:46.868
Arian -- he's a genius.
He does the cast,

00:06:46.868 --> 00:06:49.634
and then he hand-paints everyone
down to the last freckle.

00:06:49.634 --> 00:06:51.701
-And how long are you
in prosthetic for?

00:06:51.701 --> 00:06:55.567
-Well, we started at about
4 hours and 20 or 30 minutes,

00:06:55.567 --> 00:06:59.000
and he whittled it down
to under 3, like 2 1/2,

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:00.234
by the time, you know, you get
in the swing of things.

00:07:00.234 --> 00:07:01.901
-Just four hours just getting
into makeup?

00:07:01.901 --> 00:07:03.234
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-Then you have to act?

00:07:03.234 --> 00:07:04.734
-Yeah.
-No, no, no.

00:07:04.734 --> 00:07:06.667
[ Laughter ]
That's why you do what you do.

00:07:06.667 --> 00:07:08.033
-No, it's great.

00:07:08.033 --> 00:07:09.534
You can call your mom
in the chair,

00:07:09.534 --> 00:07:11.367
you get your e-mails done,
Christmas shopping.

00:07:11.367 --> 00:07:13.167
[ Laughter ]
-You got it all done, yeah.

00:07:13.167 --> 00:07:15.300
Is there a learning curve
to acting in prosthetics?

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:17.667
-Well, I didn't know that it's
a whole different skill

00:07:17.667 --> 00:07:19.334
that I didn't know
I didn't have.

00:07:19.334 --> 00:07:20.367
[ Laughter ]

00:07:20.367 --> 00:07:21.834
And I really didn't have it.

00:07:21.834 --> 00:07:23.033
I really didn't.

00:07:23.033 --> 00:07:24.667
By the time you get it on,
you're so excited,

00:07:24.667 --> 00:07:26.033
and you stand there
and then you go,

00:07:26.033 --> 00:07:29.200
"Oh, wait, I think
I'm allergic to this."

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:30.367
[ Laughter ]

00:07:30.367 --> 00:07:31.667
-You were allergic to
prosthetics?

00:07:31.667 --> 00:07:34.000
-I was so allergic
to the adhesive.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:35.501
It was hilarious.

00:07:35.501 --> 00:07:37.534
Y'all, lots of Benadryl.
Lots of Benadryl.

00:07:37.534 --> 00:07:38.934
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, my God.

00:07:38.934 --> 00:07:40.701
Benadryl knocks me out, man.

00:07:40.701 --> 00:07:42.467
Oh, my God, no way.

00:07:42.467 --> 00:07:45.400
And you still did
that knockout performance?

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:48.400
I want to say more about
"The Thing About Pam,"

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:50.901
but I can't because --
-You can't.

00:07:50.901 --> 00:07:51.968
-It's an ongoing --

00:07:51.968 --> 00:07:53.434
-So, Jimmy,
it's the weirdest thing.

00:07:53.434 --> 00:07:54.534
I get these interviews,

00:07:54.534 --> 00:07:56.968
"What interested you about
this story?"

00:07:56.968 --> 00:07:58.868
And I'm like,
"Um, nah-nah-nah, mm."

00:07:58.868 --> 00:08:01.267
-Yeah, you're like, "Ooh, uh.

00:08:01.267 --> 00:08:03.834
Kern. Kern." Exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:03.834 --> 00:08:06.601
-No, wait. It's a-a-art.
-Art, yeah.

00:08:06.601 --> 00:08:08.868
Art. Yeah.

00:08:08.868 --> 00:08:10.801
-You know, 'cause it's
an ongoing case.

00:08:10.801 --> 00:08:12.901
And, you know,
it's a real person.

00:08:12.901 --> 00:08:15.133
So I'm not allowed to
sort of speculate.

00:08:15.133 --> 00:08:17.467
Especially, you know, on
television and stuff like that.

00:08:17.467 --> 00:08:18.667
So, you get asked
these questions,

00:08:18.667 --> 00:08:20.434
and I start answering it
and then I catch myself

00:08:20.434 --> 00:08:22.367
going down the rabbit hole
and I try to correct.

00:08:22.367 --> 00:08:23.767
-Yeah, you go, "Don't stop,
just stop talking."

00:08:23.767 --> 00:08:25.501
-Yeah, you're watching yourself
talking,

00:08:25.501 --> 00:08:28.534
and you're like, "Just shut up.
Just shut your mouth."

00:08:28.534 --> 00:08:30.334
-This is so exciting.
I cannot wait for this thing.

00:08:30.334 --> 00:08:31.534
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:08:31.534 --> 00:08:33.934
Here's Renée Zellweger
in "The Thing About Pam."

00:08:33.934 --> 00:08:35.200
Take a look at this.

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:37.701
-That's how come Mariah moved in
with Betsy's mom.

00:08:39.701 --> 00:08:41.834
-Was he ever violent with Betsy?

00:08:41.834 --> 00:08:46.434
-I was at their place a lot,
and, um, he's not --

00:08:46.434 --> 00:08:49.901
he's not the most, um...

00:08:49.901 --> 00:08:55.934
He was kind of not nice verbally
to her.

00:08:55.934 --> 00:08:57.467
Mm.

00:08:58.968 --> 00:09:01.234
You know the type.

00:09:01.234 --> 00:09:03.567
Real aggressive.

00:09:03.567 --> 00:09:04.567
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Wow!

